infinite-right-handed-neutrino-mass-limi.interpretation:
In the Type I Seesaw model, the active neutrino mass matrix is given by the approximation $m_{
u} \approx -m_D M_R^{-1} m_D^T$, where $m_D$ is the Dirac mass matrix and $M_R$ is the Majorana mass matrix for the right-handed neutrinos. In the simplified single-generation case, this becomes $m_{
u} \approx -m_D^2 / M_R$. The limit $M_R ightarrow \infty$ implies that for a fixed Dirac mass $m_D$ (typically assumed to be of the same order as other lepton masses), the active neutrino mass $m_{
u}$ approaches zero. This provides a compelling dynamical explanation for the observed smallness of neutrino masses, as the heavy right-handed neutrinos decouple from the low-energy effective theory, leaving behind only the very light active neutrino mass eigenstates.
